<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Are the patterns you noticed still happening? See if you can apply those patterns to the following equations.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis>-3
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -5
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), does the graph open up or down? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient \((\frac{1}{2})\).
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>-100
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>50
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), does the graph open up or down? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The graph opens downward. The squared term has a negative coefficient \((-1)\).
  </item>
</list>
